<!-- ========== SECTION 1: GLOBAL OVERVIEW ========== -->
<div class="section" id="global-overview">
  <h2>1. Global Homicide Rate Overview</h2>

  <p>Over the 34-year observation window from 1990 to 2023, intentional homicide rates show a broadly encouraging but uneven trajectory. The <strong>global mean rate</strong> declined from approximately 8.3 per 100,000 in the 1990s to 7.7 in the 2010s — a decrease of about 9.6% between the 2000s and 2010s. However, the 2020s data so far shows the mean rate stabilizing at around 7.7, suggesting the pace of improvement has stalled.</p>

  <p>The <strong>median rate</strong> — a more robust measure less influenced by extreme outliers — has shown a more consistent downward trend, falling from roughly 4.0 per 100k in the 1990s to 2.3 in the 2020s. This indicates that for the typical country, safety from homicide has measurably improved.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {img_tag(chart_trend, "Global homicide rate trend")}
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Key Finding:</strong> The gap between mean and median rates has widened over time, suggesting that while most countries improved, a subset of high-homicide nations became more extreme — pulling the mean upward relative to the median.
  </div>

  <h3>Decade-over-Decade Comparison</h3>
  <p>The decade-level aggregation confirms the non-linear trajectory of global homicide trends. The 1990s-to-2000s shift showed a marginal increase in mean rates ({dec_90_00}), likely driven by the expansion of reporting to include more high-homicide countries. The 2000s-to-2010s shift saw meaningful improvement ({dec_00_10}), while the 2010s-to-2020s comparison shows near-stagnation ({dec_10_20}), though this is partially driven by reduced coverage in the most recent years.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {img_tag(chart_decades, "Decade comparison")}
  </div>

  <h3>Reporting Coverage</h3>
  <p>Data availability is a critical dimension. Coverage peaked at {int(max(R['coverage']['n']))} countries in {R['coverage']['year'][R['coverage']['n'].index(max(R['coverage']['n']))]} and has since declined, with only 84 countries reporting in 2023. This uneven coverage means global aggregates should be interpreted with caution, particularly for recent years.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {img_tag(chart_coverage, "Reporting coverage")}
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ========== SECTION 2: REGIONAL COMPARISON ========== -->
<div class="section" id="regional-comparison">
  <h2>2. Regional &amp; Country-Level Comparison</h2>

  <p>Homicide rates vary enormously by region. The Americas consistently record the highest median rates — typically 5–12× higher than Europe. Africa's available data also shows elevated rates, though coverage is notably sparse. Asia and Oceania cluster at relatively low levels, while Europe has maintained the lowest median rates throughout the observation period.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {img_tag(chart_regional, "Regional trends")}
  </div>

  <h3>Regional Summary ({R['recent_year']})</h3>
  {reg_tbl}

  <div class="callout warning">
    <strong>Coverage Caveat:</strong> Africa's regional statistics are based on only 10 reporting countries in {R['recent_year']}, making the figures less representative of the full continent. The Americas and Europe have much better coverage.
  </div>

  <h3>Top 10 Highest and Lowest Rates ({R['recent_year']})</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    {img_tag(chart_top_bottom, "Top and bottom countries")}
  </div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <h3>Highest Rates</h3>
      {top10_tbl}
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Lowest Rates</h3>
      {bot10_tbl}
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>The concentration of the highest homicide rates in Caribbean and Central American nations is striking. Eight of the ten highest-rate countries in {R['recent_year']} are in the Caribbean basin or Central America. At the other extreme, several East Asian and Middle Eastern states record rates below 0.3 per 100k, with the Holy See recording a rate of zero.</p>
</div>

<!-- ========== SECTION 3: GENDER DISPARITY ========== -->
<div class="section" id="sex-disparity">
  <h2>3. Gender Disparity in Homicide Victimization</h2>

  <p>Intentional homicide is profoundly gendered. Across the global dataset, <strong>men are consistently victimized at rates far exceeding women</strong>. In {R['sex_year']}, the median male homicide rate stood at {kpi['male_rate']:.2f} per 100k, compared to {kpi['female_rate']:.2f} for females — a <strong>ratio of {kpi['mf_ratio']:.1f}:1</strong>.</p>

  <div class="kpi-dashboard" style="max-width:500px;">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value" style="color:#2980b9">{kpi['male_rate']:.2f}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Male Median Rate</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value" style="color:#e74c3c">{kpi['female_rate']:.2f}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Female Median Rate</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{kpi['mf_ratio']:.1f}×</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Male:Female Ratio</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {img_tag(chart_gender, "Gender disparity over time")}
  </div>

  <p>The male-to-female ratio has fluctuated between approximately 2.5× and 4.5× over the observation period. While both male and female rates have generally declined, the ratio has shown no consistent convergence — the gender gap in homicide victimization remains a persistent structural feature of violence globally.</p>

  <h3>Country-Level Gender Disparity</h3>
  <p>Some countries show extreme gender disparities in homicide rates, with male-to-female ratios exceeding 8:1 or even 10:1. These tend to be nations where gang violence, organized crime, or armed conflict disproportionately claim male victims.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {img_tag(chart_gender_countries, "Country gender disparities")}
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Policy Implication:</strong> While men face higher absolute homicide risk, female homicide victimization often occurs in domestic contexts — a pattern that aggregate rates can obscure. Sex-disaggregated data is essential for designing targeted prevention strategies.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ========== SECTION 4: AGE GROUP ========== -->
<div class="section" id="age-group-analysis">
  <h2>4. Age Group Analysis</h2>

  <p>Homicide risk is not evenly distributed across age groups. The data reveals a clear vulnerability peak in young adulthood, with the <strong>25–29 age group</strong> recording the highest mean homicide rate globally at {age_tbl_data[5]['mean']:.2f} per 100k, followed closely by the 20–24 group ({age_tbl_data[4]['mean']:.2f}). Rates then decline progressively with age.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {img_tag(chart_age, "Age group analysis")}
  </div>

  {age_tbl}

  <p>Children under 10 and those aged 10–14 face the lowest rates, though any homicide of a child represents a severe concern. The sharp escalation beginning at age 15–17 (mean rate of {age_tbl_data[2]['mean']:.2f}) marks a critical transition point that merits focused prevention efforts.</p>

  <h3>Age and Sex Interaction</h3>
  <p>The age-risk profile differs dramatically by sex. Young males (18–29) face homicide rates many times higher than young females of the same age, while the gender gap narrows substantially for older age groups and for children.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {img_tag(chart_age_sex, "Age and sex interaction")}
  </div>

  <div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Critical Finding:</strong> Males aged 20–29 constitute the highest-risk demographic globally. Prevention programs targeting this group — including employment initiatives, anti-gang interventions, and conflict resolution — are likely to yield the highest return on investment.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ========== SECTION 5: NOTABLE TRENDS ========== -->
<div class="section" id="notable-trends">
  <h2>5. Notable Country-Level Trends &amp; Outliers</h2>

  <p>While global aggregates suggest gradual improvement, the country-level picture is far more varied. Some nations achieved remarkable reductions, while others saw dramatic deteriorations.</p>

  <h3>Most Improved: Largest Absolute Decreases</h3>
  {big_dec_tbl}

  <p><strong>El Salvador</strong> stands out as the most dramatic success story, with its homicide rate falling from 134.3 per 100k in 1994 to 7.9 in 2022 — a 94% reduction. <strong>Colombia's</strong> rate dropped from 75.0 to 24.9 over the same broad period, reflecting sustained security reforms. <strong>South Africa</strong> also saw meaningful improvement, though its current rate of 43.7 remains extremely elevated.</p>

  <h3>Most Deteriorated: Largest Absolute Increases</h3>
  {big_inc_tbl}

  <p>Several Caribbean and Central American nations saw steep increases. <strong>Ecuador's</strong> rate surged from 8.5 to 45.7, a five-fold increase driven by drug-trafficking related violence in recent years. <strong>Haiti's</strong> political instability contributed to rates rising from 5.2 to 41.2. <strong>Jamaica</strong> saw its already-high rate climb from 22.9 to 49.4.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {img_tag(chart_outliers, "Country outlier trends")}
  </div>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <strong>Note:</strong> The Turks and Caicos Islands shows an extreme increase (0 to 76.3), but this likely reflects its very small population (&lt;50,000), where a small number of homicides produces large per-capita swings. Small-population territories should be interpreted with caution.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ========== SECTION 6: LIMITATIONS & RECOMMENDATIONS ========== -->
<div class="section" id="data-limitations-recommendations">
  <h2>6. Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</h2>

  <h3>Data Limitations</h3>
  <ul class="findings">
    <li><strong>Unbalanced panel:</strong> This dataset is not a complete panel — not all countries report in every year. The set of reporting countries changes annually, which means trends in global aggregates may reflect compositional shifts (which countries report) as much as actual changes in violence. In 2023, only 84 of 201 countries reported data.</li>
    <li><strong>No interpolation:</strong> Missing years are left as gaps, not imputed. Analysts should avoid naive year-over-year comparisons without verifying that the underlying country set is consistent.</li>
    <li><strong>United Kingdom:</strong> UK data refer to England and Wales only, as reported by the UNODC. Scotland and Northern Ireland may appear as separate entries. This means the "United Kingdom" label understates the country's geographic scope.</li>
    <li><strong>Small territories:</strong> Several territories with very small populations (e.g., Turks and Caicos, Anguilla, Holy See) can produce extreme per-capita rates from a small number of events. These should be flagged or excluded in high-level comparisons.</li>
    <li><strong>Regional mapping:</strong> Region assignments are based on ISO codes and may group geographically proximate but socially distinct territories. Africa's severe underrepresentation (only 10 countries in {R['recent_year']}) limits the reliability of regional conclusions for that continent.</li>
    <li><strong>Age-group data:</strong> Age-disaggregated rates are only available for male and female categories separately (not "both"), and not all countries report age breakdowns, reducing the sample size for demographic analysis.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>

  <div class="callout success">
    <strong>1. Prioritize the Americas and Sub-Saharan Africa.</strong> These regions consistently show the highest homicide rates. Resource allocation for crime prevention, institutional strengthening, and community-based violence intervention should be concentrated here. The Caribbean basin in particular requires urgent attention.
  </div>

  <div class="callout success">
    <strong>2. Target young males aged 15–29.</strong> This demographic faces the highest homicide risk globally. Programs combining employment opportunities, education, mentorship, and conflict resolution have shown evidence of effectiveness and should be scaled.
  </div>

  <div class="callout success">
    <strong>3. Invest in sex-disaggregated data collection.</strong> While the overall male:female ratio is well-established, understanding the context of female homicide victimization (e.g., domestic violence, femicide) requires more granular data than aggregate rates alone can provide.
  </div>

  <div class="callout success">
    <strong>4. Strengthen reporting infrastructure.</strong> The declining coverage rate — from a peak of {int(max(R['coverage']['n']))} countries to just 84 in 2023 — undermines the ability to monitor global trends. International cooperation to build statistical capacity in underreporting nations is essential.
  </div>

  <div class="callout success">
    <strong>5. Investigate success stories.</strong> Nations like El Salvador and Colombia achieved dramatic reductions. Systematic case studies of their approaches — including security reforms, gang intervention programs, and institutional changes — could yield transferable lessons for other high-homicide settings.
  </div>

  <div class="callout success">
    <strong>6. Account for small-population effects.</strong> Future analyses should either weight by population or exclude territories below a population threshold to avoid misleading per-capita rates.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Footer -->
